" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture1-Introduction · slide 33/35</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Process monitoring<br>Dashboards, alerts &amp; reports<br>Model-based analytics (p. mining)<br>Event<br>stream<br>DB logs<br>33</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d01/034.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture1-Introduction · slide 34: Course structure\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture1-Introduction · slide 34/35</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Course structure<br>Weeks 2 &amp; 4<br>Weeks 5-7<br>Weeks 8-9<br>Weeks 10-11<br>Weeks 12-14<br>35<br>Week 3<br>Gover-nance<br>Culture<br>Strategy</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d01/035.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture1-Introduction · slide 35: Fundamentals of Business Process Management\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture1-Introduction · slide 35/35</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Fundamentals of Business Process Management<br>Chapter 1 – Introduction<br>Further Readings &amp; Resources<br>36</p></div>\n<div class=\"ml-en\"><h3>📑 School slides — Lecture2-Identification (38 slides)</h3>\n<p class=\"ghi-chu\">matched by content (deck title ↔ session topic). File: Lecture2-Identification.pptx</p></div>\n<div class=\"ml-vi\"><h3>📑 Slide của trường — Lecture2-Identification (38 slide)</h3>\n<p class=\"ghi-chu\">ghép theo nội dung (tên deck ↔ chủ đề buổi). Tệp: Lecture2-Identification.pptx</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/001.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 1: Lecture 2Process Identification\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 1/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Lecture 2Process Identification<br>1</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/002.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 2: Learning Objectives\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 2/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Learning Objectives<br>Understand key concepts of business processes, and the importance of systematically managing such processes to improve organizational performance<br>Identify and document business processes at different levels of detail using contemporary process modelling techniques<br>2</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/003.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 3: Content\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 3/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Content<br>Process Identification<br>Understand the importance of process identification<br>Definition of the Process Architecture.<br>Understand Process Selection<br>Exercise 2<br>3</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/004.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 4: What?\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 4/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>What?<br>Identify an organization’s business processes<br>Prioritize their management based on certain criteria<br>Why?<br>Understand the organization<br>Maximize value of BPM projects<br>Process identification</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/005.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 5: Designation step\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 5/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Designation step<br>Enumerate main processes<br>Determine process scope<br>Prioritization step (aka Process selection)<br>Prioritize processes based on:<br>Importance<br>Health<br>Feasibility<br>Process identification steps<br>After Davenport (1993)<br>Process Architecture<br>PrioritizedProcessPortfolio</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/006.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 6: “Most businesses have just three core processes:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 6/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>“Most businesses have just three core processes:<br>Sell stuff<br>Deliver stuff<br>Making sure you have stuff to sell and deliver”<br>Geary Rummler<br>Process Enumeration</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/007.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 7: Porter: Types of processes\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 7/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Porter: Types of processes<br>Core Processes<br>Management Processes<br>Suppliers / Partners<br>Customers / Stakeholders<br>Support Processes<br>After Michael Porter (1985)</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/008.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 8: Core processes\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 8/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Core processes<br>Sales (lead-to-quote, quote-to-order, order-to-cash)<br>Purchase-to-Pay (direct procurement, e.g. supplies replenishment)<br>…<br>Support processes<br>Purchase-to-pay (indirect procurement, e.g. parts replenishment, operational resources replenishment…)<br>HR (policies update, recruitment, induction, probation…)<br>…<br>Management processes<br>Suppliers management (suppliers planning, suppliers acquisition…)<br>Logistics management (logistics planning, logistics controlling…)<br>…<br>Example: core, support and management processes<br>Grocery Wholesaler</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/009.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 9: Relations between core, support, mgt processes\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 9/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Relations between core, support, mgt processes</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/010.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 10: StrategicManagement\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 10/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>StrategicManagement<br>LogisticsManagement<br>WarehouseManagement<br>SuppliersManagement<br>Management processes<br>Finance<br>Indirect<br>procurement<br>IT<br>HR<br>Core processes<br>Support processes<br>Wholesaler<br>Example: process architecture<br>DemandManagement<br>Sales<br>Direct<br>procurement<br>Distribution<br>Service<br>Marketing<br>Process group</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/011.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 11: StrategicManagement\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 11/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>StrategicManagement<br>CorporateDevelopment<br>InvestorRelations<br>Risk Assessment &amp; Management<br>MarketDevelopment<br>Management processes<br>Marketing &amp; Sales<br>Underwriting<br>Management<br>Policy   Servicing<br>ClaimsManagement<br>Payments Collection and Disbursement<br>Assets Management<br>Finance/<br>Treasury<br>Legal/<br>Audit<br>Reinsurance<br>IT<br>HR<br>Core processes<br>Support processes<br>Insurance company<br>Example: process architecture</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/012.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 12: These groups of processes are typically performed at a university. Categorize each process group as core, support or management\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 12/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>These groups of processes are typically performed at a university. Categorize each process group as core, support or management<br>Exercise: classify by process type<br>StrategicManagement<br>CurriculumManagement<br>IPManagement<br>Additionalservices mgt<br>Indirect<br>procurement<br>IT<br>HR<br>Marketing<br>Market<br>management<br>Teachingdegree courses<br>Teaching professional courses<br>Language<br>training<br>Sport<br>services<br>Admission</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/013.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 13: Solution: identify process types\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 13/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Solution: identify process types</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/014.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 14: Processes are interdependent  insights into interrelations required\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 14/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Processes are interdependent  insights into interrelations required<br>Specialization: general – special product/service<br>Horizontal: upstream – downstream processes and their value chains<br>Vertical: main processes – sub-processes<br>Process scoping<br>Process architecture</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/015.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 15: Chain of processes an organization performs to deliver value to customers and stakeholders\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 15/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Chain of processes an organization performs to deliver value to customers and stakeholders<br>More generally, a mechanism to group high-level business processes according to an order relation (can be applied to core, support and management processes)<br>Value chain modeling<br>Procure-to-service<br>business<br>process<br>orderrelation</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/016.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 16: Change of key business object in the process\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 16/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Change of key business object in the process<br>Change of granularity of main business object<br>Change in frequency/time<br>Change in intermediate outcome/resolution/objective<br>Guidelines to identify horizontal boundaries in value chains</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/017.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 17: Example: value chain\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 17/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Example: value chain<br>Wholesaler<br>Core processes</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/018.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 18: Think around three main steps:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 18/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Think around three main steps:<br>Imagine it (design new product/service)<br>Build it (source, assemble, deliver product/service)<br>Sell it (market, sell, service product/service)<br>Typical value chains for core processes<br>Example: Producer<br>Stockedproducts:<br>MTOproducts:<br>ETOproducts:<br>Specializations</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/019.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 19: Support processes\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 19/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Support processes<br>HR:<br>Accounting:<br>Management processes<br>Suppliers<br>management:<br>Risk<br>management:<br>Example: value chain of non-core processes</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/020.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 20: StrategicManagement\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 20/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>StrategicManagement<br>WarehouseManagement<br>SuppliersManagement<br>LogisticsManagement<br>Management processes<br>Procure-to-Service<br>Finance<br>Indirect<br>procurement<br>IT<br>HR<br>Core processes<br>Support processes<br>Wholesaler<br>Example: process architecture &amp; value chains<br>DemandManagement<br>Box:groups<br>processes/value chains<br>Chevron:collapsedvalue chain<br>Sales<br>Direct<br>procurement<br>Distribution<br>Service<br>Marketing</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/021.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 21: Alternative: process architecture – groups\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 21/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Alternative: process architecture – groups<br>Consultancy Firm<br>Expanded<br>process group</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/022.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 22: Value chains\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 22/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Value chains<br>Chains of processes. Stay at a high level. Rule of thumb: 3-7 processes<br>Procure-to-service, Risk management<br>(Root/Main) Processes<br>Build up value chains and affect each other. They are abstract<br>Lead-to-quote, Quote-to-order, Order-to-cash<br>Subprocesses<br>These are detailed, involve multiple activities and can be layered on different levels.<br>Order shipment, invoicing<br>Tasks<br>These are atomic and performed by human resources, IT systems or equipment<br>E.g. Approve invoice<br>Typical artifacts for vertical scoping<br>Typical focus of Process enumeration</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/023.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 23: Process architecture: hierarchical view\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 23/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Process architecture: hierarchical view<br>Process hierarchy<br>Level 1<br>Process<br>Landscape<br>Level 2<br>MainProcesses<br>(e.g. BPMN)<br>Level 3+<br>Subprocesses, Tasks<br>(e.g. BPMN)</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/024.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 24: How many levels in the process architecture?\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 24/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>How many levels in the process architecture?<br>25</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/025.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 25: Example: hierarchical process architecture\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 25/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Example: hierarchical process architecture<br>Level 1<br>Insurance company<br>StrategicManagement<br>CorporateDevelopment<br>InvestorRelations<br>Risk Assessment &amp; Management<br>MarketDevelopment<br>Management processes<br>Marketing &amp; Sales<br>Underwriting<br>Management<br>Policy   Servicing<br>ClaimsManagement<br>Payments Collection and Disbursement<br>Assets Management<br>Finance/<br>Treasury<br>Legal/<br>Audit<br>Reinsurance<br>IT<br>HR<br>Core processes<br>Support processes</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/026.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 26: Strategic\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 26/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Strategic<br>Management<br>Corporate<br>development<br>Marketing<br>development<br>Investor<br>relations<br>Management processes<br>Define<br>business concept &amp;<br>long-term vision<br>Develop<br>business<br>strategy<br>Design &amp; manage<br>corporate policies<br>Manage<br>knowledge<br>Plan<br>investor<br>relations<br>Build<br>investor<br>relations<br>Manage<br>investor<br>relations<br>Communicate<br>with stakeholders<br>Manage<br>strategic<br>initiatives<br>Define offeringand customervalue proposition<br>Define pricing<br>strategy<br>Define and manage<br>channel strategy<br>Manage<br>change &amp;improvement<br>Manage<br>partners<br>Manage<br>investments &amp;<br>divestments<br>Process<br>group<br>Process<br>subgroup<br>Level 2<br>Example: hierarchical process architecture<br>Insurance company</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/027.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 27: Develop\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 27/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Develop<br>businessstrategy<br>Management processes<br>Evaluate strategic<br>options<br>Select<br>long-term business<br>strategy<br>Coordinate<br>functional andoperational strategies<br>Develop overall<br>mission statement<br>Define offeringand customervalue proposition<br>Develop value<br>proposition<br>Validate valueproposition<br>Develop<br>new branding<br>Define<br>offering and<br>positioning<br>Align<br>functional andoperational strategies<br>Createorganisational<br>design<br>Developorganisational<br>goals<br>Formulate<br>business unit<br>strategies<br>Process<br>subgroup<br>Main<br>process<br>Level 3<br>Example: process architecture<br>Insurance company</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/028.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 28: Develop overall\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 28/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Develop overall<br>mission statement<br>Management processes<br>Formulate<br>mission<br>Communicate<br>mission<br>Define<br>current business<br>Main<br>process<br>Subprocess<br>Level 4<br>Example: hierarchical process architecture<br>Insurance company</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/029.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 29: A reference model is used as a template to design the process architecture\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 29/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>A reference model is used as a template to design the process architecture<br>Examples:<br>Information Technology Infrastructure Library (ITIL)<br>Supply Chain Operations Reference Model (SCOR)<br>Process Classification Framework (PCF)<br>Control Objectives for Information Technology (COBIT)<br>Value Reference Model (VRM)<br>Voluntary Interindustry Commerce Solutions (VICS)<br>eTOM Business Process Framework<br>Designation via reference models</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/030.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 30: Industry-neutral enterprise model\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 30/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Industry-neutral enterprise model<br>Open standard for benchmarking<br>Four levels<br>Categories<br>Process group<br>Process<br>Activity<br>Example: APQC Process Classification Framework (PCF)</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/031.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 31: APQC PCF Overview\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 31/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>APQC PCF Overview<br>Category</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/032.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 32: APQC Classification Framework\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 32/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>APQC Classification Framework<br>Group<br>Process<br>Activity</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/033.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 33: Importance\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 33/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Importance<br>Which processes have greatest impact on the organization‘s strategic objectives?<br>Health (or Dysfunction)<br>Which processes are in deepest trouble?<br>Feasibility<br>Which processes are most susceptible to successful process management?<br>Prioritized process portfolio<br>Prioritization (aka Process Selection)<br>Hammer, Champy (1993)</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/034.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 34: Financial institution\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 34/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Financial institution<br>Example: prioritized process portfolio<br>Health<br>Importance<br>High<br>Low<br>Good<br>Poor<br>Short-term action<br>Rating<br>Contract<br>preparation<br>Loan market<br>evaluation<br>Handling of<br>payments<br>Loan<br>application<br>Loan<br>planning<br>Loan<br>controlling<br>Loan<br>decision<br>Feasibility<br>Low<br>High<br>Medium<br>Possible<br>Strategic fit?</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/035.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 35: Importance\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 35/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Importance<br>Which processes have greatest impact on the organization‘s strategic objectives?<br>Health (or Dysfunction)<br>Which processes are in deepest trouble?<br>Feasibility<br>Which processes are most susceptible to successful process management?<br>Prioritized process portfolio<br>Prioritization<br>Hammer, Champy (1993)</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/036.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 36: Financial institution\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 36/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Financial institution<br>Example: prioritized process PICK chart<br>Health<br>Importance<br>High<br>Low<br>Good<br>Poor<br>Short-term action<br>Rating<br>Contract<br>preparation<br>Loan market<br>evaluation<br>Handling of<br>payments<br>Loan<br>application<br>Loan<br>planning<br>Loan<br>controlling<br>Loan<br>decision<br>Feasibility<br>Low<br>High<br>Medium<br>Possible<br>Strategic fit?</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/037.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 37: Fundamentals of Business Process Management\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 37/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Fundamentals of Business Process Management<br>Chapter 2 – Process Identification<br>Further Readings &amp; Resources<br>38</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d02/038.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture2-Identification · slide 38: Process Modeling\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture2-Identification · slide 38/38</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Process Modeling<br>Next lesson<br>39</p></div>\n<div class=\"ml-en\"><h3>📑 School slides — Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 (49 slides)</h3>\n<p class=\"ghi-chu\">matched by content (deck title ↔ session topic). File: Lecture3-ProcessModeling1.pptx</p></div>\n<div class=\"ml-vi\"><h3>📑 Slide của trường — Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 (49 slide)</h3>\n<p class=\"ghi-chu\">ghép theo nội dung (tên deck ↔ chủ đề buổi). Tệp: Lecture3-ProcessModeling1.pptx</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/001.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 1: Lecture 3\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 1/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Lecture 3<br>Process Modelling (part 1)<br>1</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/002.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 2: Learning Objectives\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 2/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Learning Objectives<br>Understand key concepts of business processes, and the importance of systematically managing such processes to improve organizational performance<br>Identify and document business processes at different levels of detail using contemporary process modelling techniques<br>2</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/003.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 3: Content\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 3/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Content<br>Process Modelling<br>Describe the essential concepts of process models, namely how process models relate to process instances.<br>Definition of the Process Architecture.<br>Explain the four main structural blocks of branching and merging in processmodels. These define exclusive decisions, parallel execution, inclusive decisions,and repetition<br>Learn how to use sub-processes to reducethe model’s complexity, and how to reuse these sub-process models from withindifferent process models<br>Exercise 3 -4<br>3</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/004.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 4: Course structure\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 4/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Course structure<br>4<br>Weeks 3-4<br>Weeks 5-7<br>Weeks 8-9<br>Weeks 10-11<br>Weeks 12-14<br>Week 2</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/005.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 5: Purposes of process modeling\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 5/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Purposes of process modeling<br>5<br>Communication<br>Documentation<br>Analysis (e.g. simulation)</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/006.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 6: Business Process Model and Notation (BPMN)\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 6/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Business Process Model and Notation (BPMN)<br>OMG standard<br>Suitable for capturing models for process discovery, analysis, and implementation<br>Supported by numerous tools, incl.<br>Apromore – available for free to students in this course (see Slack)<br>GBTEC BIC Design<br>Bizagi Process Modeler (free)<br>Signavio (academic version: academic.signavio.com)</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/007.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 7: BPMN from 10,000 miles…\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 7/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>BPMN from 10,000 miles…<br>A BPMN process model is a graph consisting of four types of core elements:<br>activity<br>gateway<br>event<br>sequence flow<br>start<br>end</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/008.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 8: An order-to-cash process is triggered by the receipt of a purchase order from a customer. Upon receipt, the purchase order has to be…\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 8/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>An order-to-cash process is triggered by the receipt of a purchase order from a customer. Upon receipt, the purchase order has to be checked against the stock to determine if the the requested item(s) are available. Depending on stock availability the purchase order may be confirmed or rejected.<br>If the purchase order is confirmed, an invoice is emitted and the goods requested are shipped. The process completes by archiving the order.<br>Let’s start modeling<br>Order-to-cash</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/009.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 9: An order-to-cash process is triggered by the receipt of a purchase order from a customer.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 9/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>An order-to-cash process is triggered by the receipt of a purchase order from a customer.<br>Upon receipt, the purchase order has to be checked against the stock to determine if the the requested item(s) are available.<br>Depending on stock availability the purchase order may be confirmed or rejected.<br>If the purchase order is confirmed, an invoice is emitted and the goods requested are shipped. The process completes by archiving the order.<br>Let’s start modeling – break it down<br>Order-to-cash</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/010.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 10: An order-to-cash process is triggered by the receipt of a purchase order from a customer.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 10/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>An order-to-cash process is triggered by the receipt of a purchase order from a customer.<br>Upon receipt, the purchase order has to be checked against the stock to determine if the the requested item(s) are available.<br>Let’s start modeling – break it down<br>Order-to-cash</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/011.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 11: BPMN Model\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 11/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>BPMN Model<br>11<br>Order-to-cash</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/012.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 12: An order-to-cash process is triggered by the receipt of a purchase order from a customer.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 12/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>An order-to-cash process is triggered by the receipt of a purchase order from a customer.<br>Upon receipt, the purchase order has to be checked against the stock to determine if the the requested item(s) are available.<br>Depending on stock availability the purchase order may be confirmed or rejected.<br>If the purchase order is confirmed, an invoice is emitted and the goods requested are shipped. The process completes by archiving the order.<br>Let’s start modeling – break it down<br>Order-to-cash</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/013.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 13: BPMN Model\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 13/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>BPMN Model<br>startevent<br>endevent<br>activity<br>split gateway<br>13<br>Order-to-cash<br>endevent<br>Event: noun + past-participle verb (e.g. insurance claim lodged)<br>Naming conventions<br>Activity: verb + noun (e.g. assess credit risk)</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/014.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 14: Execution of a process model The “token game”\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 14/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Execution of a process model The “token game”<br>14<br>Order #1<br>Order #2<br>Order #3</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/015.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 15: A start event triggers a new process instanceby generating a token that traverses the sequence flow (“tokens source”)\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 15/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>A start event triggers a new process instanceby generating a token that traverses the sequence flow (“tokens source”)<br>An end event signals that a process instance has<br>completed with a given outcome by consuming<br>a token (“tokens sink”)<br>A little bit more on events…<br>15<br>start<br>event<br>end<br>event</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/016.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 16: […] If the purchase order is confirmed, an invoice is emitted and the goods requested are shipped (in any order). The process comple…\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 16/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>[…] If the purchase order is confirmed, an invoice is emitted and the goods requested are shipped (in any order). The process completes by archiving the order. […]<br>Order-to-cash example revisited…<br>16</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/017.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 17: First try\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 17/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>First try<br>split<br>split<br>join<br>17<br>Order-to-cash</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/018.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 18: XOR-split  takes one outgoing branch\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 18/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>XOR-split  takes one outgoing branch<br>XOR-join  proceeds when one incoming branch has completed<br>A little more on gateways: XOR Gateway<br>18<br>An XOR Gateway captures decision points (XOR-split) and points where alternative flows are merged (XOR-join)</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/019.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 19: Example: XOR Gateway\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 19/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Example: XOR Gateway<br>5<br>Invoice checking process</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/020.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 20: A little more on gateways: AND Gateway\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 20/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>A little more on gateways: AND Gateway<br>20<br>An AND Gateway provides a mechanism to create and synchronize “parallel” flows.<br>AND-split  takes all outgoing branches<br>AND-join  proceeds when all incoming branches have completed</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/021.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 21: 21\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 21/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>21<br>Example: AND Gateway<br>Airport security check</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/022.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 22: Revised order-to-cash process model\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 22/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Revised order-to-cash process model<br>XOR-split<br>AND-split<br>AND-join<br>22</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/023.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 23: A company has two warehouses that store different products: Amsterdam and Hamburg. When an order is received, it is distributed acro…\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 23/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>A company has two warehouses that store different products: Amsterdam and Hamburg. When an order is received, it is distributed across these warehouses: if some of the relevant products are maintained in Amsterdam, a sub-order is sent there; likewise, if some relevant products are maintained in Hamburg, a sub-order is sent there. Afterwards, the order is registered and the process completes.<br>Between XOR and AND<br>23<br>Order distribution process</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/024.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 24: Solution 1\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 24/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Solution 1<br>XOR-split<br>XOR-join<br>AND-split<br>AND-join<br>24<br>Order distribution process</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/025.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 25: Solution 2\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 25/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Solution 2<br>25<br>Order distribution process<br>XOR-split<br>XOR-join<br>AND-split<br>AND-join</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/026.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 26: OR Gateway\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 26/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>OR Gateway<br>26<br>An OR Gateway provides a mechanism to create and synchronize n out of m parallel flows.<br>OR-split  takes one or more branches depending on conditions<br>OR-join  proceeds when all active incoming branches have completed</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/027.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 27: Solution using OR Gateway\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 27/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Solution using OR Gateway<br>OR-split<br>OR-join<br>27<br>Order distribution process</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/028.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 28: What join type do we need here?\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 28/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>What join type do we need here?<br>28</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/029.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 29: Beware: Beginner’s Mistake…\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 29/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Beware: Beginner’s Mistake…</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/030.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 30: Guidelines: Naming Conventions\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 30/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Guidelines: Naming Conventions<br>Give a name to every event and task<br>For tasks: verb followed by business object name and possibly a  complement<br>Issue Driver Licence, Renew Licence via Agency<br>For message events: object + past participle<br>Invoice received, Claim settled<br>Avoid generic verbs such as Handle, Record…<br>Label each XOR-split with a condition<br>Policy is invalid, Claim is inadmissible</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/031.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 31: Poll: Which model do you prefer?\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 31/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Poll: Which model do you prefer?</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/032.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 32: One more guideline…\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 32/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>One more guideline…<br>Model in blocks<br>Pair up each AND-split with an AND-join and each XOR-split with a XOR-join, whenever possible<br>Exception: sometimes a XOR-split leads to two end events – different outcomes (cf. order management example)</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/033.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 33: Rework and repetition\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 33/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Rework and repetition<br>Address ministerial correspondence<br>In the minister’s office, when a ministerial inquiry has been received, it is registered into the system. Then the inquiry is investigated so that a ministerial response can be prepared.<br>The finalization of a response includes the preparation of the response itself by the cabinet officer and the review of the response by the principal registrar. If the registrar does not approve the response, the latter needs to be prepared again by the cabinet officer for review. The process finishes only once the response has been approved.<br>XOR-join: entry point<br>XOR-split: exit point</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/034.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 34: Quick Note: Implicit vs. explicit gateways\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 34/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Quick Note: Implicit vs. explicit gateways<br>34</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/035.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 35: How this process starts? How it ends?\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 35/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>How this process starts? How it ends?<br>35</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/036.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 36: What’s wrong with this model? How to fix it?\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 36/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>What’s wrong with this model? How to fix it?<br>X</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/037.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 37: When?\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 37/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>When?<br>Flows<br>Gateways<br>Which?<br>Data / Materials<br>What?<br>Tasks<br>Events<br>Who?<br>Organization<br>Process Modelling Viewpoints<br>Lanes &amp;<br>Pools<br>Data Objects, Stores</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/038.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 38: Organizational Elements in BPMN – Pools &amp; Lanes\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 38/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Organizational Elements in BPMN – Pools &amp; Lanes<br>38<br>Pool<br>Captures a resource class. Generally used to model a business party (e.g. a whole company)<br>Lane<br>A resource sub-class within a pool. Generally used to model departments (e.g. shipping, finance), internal roles (e.g. Manager, Associate), software systems (e.g. ERP, CRM)</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/039.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 39: Order-to-cash process with lanes\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 39/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Order-to-cash process with lanes</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/040.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 40: A Message Flow represents a flow of information between two process parties (Pools)\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 40/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>A Message Flow represents a flow of information between two process parties (Pools)<br>Message Flow<br>40<br>A Message Flow can connect:<br>directly to the boundary of a Pool  captures an informative message to/from that party<br>to a specific activity or event within that Pool  captures a message that triggers a specific activity/event within that party</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/041.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 41: Order-to-cash process with a black-box customer pool\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 41/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Order-to-cash process with a black-box customer pool<br>41</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/042.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 42: A Sequence Flow cannot cross the boundaries of a Pool (message flows can)\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 42/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>A Sequence Flow cannot cross the boundaries of a Pool (message flows can)<br>Both Sequence Flow and Message Flow can cross the boundaries of Lanes<br>A Message Flow cannot connect two flow elements within the same pool<br>Pools, Lanes and Flows: syntactic rules</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/043.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 43: One more guideline…\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 43/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>One more guideline…<br>Start modeling with one single “white-box” pool<br>Initially, put the events and tasks in only one pool – the pool of the party who is running the process<br>Leave all other pools “black-boxed”<br>Once you have modeled this way, and once the process diagram inside the white-box pool is complete, you can model the details (events and tasks) in the other pools if that is useful.<br>In this course we will only model processes with one single white-box pool – all other pools are black-box</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/044.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 44: Which?\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 44/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Which?<br>Data / Materials<br>Process Modelling Viewpoints<br>Data Objects, Stores</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/045.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 45: A Data Object captures an artifact required (input) or produced (output) by an activity.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 45/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>A Data Object captures an artifact required (input) or produced (output) by an activity.<br>Can be physical or electronic<br>BPMN Information Artifacts<br>45<br>A Data Store is a place containing data objects that must be persisted beyond the duration of a process instance.<br>It is used by an activity to store (as output) or retrieve (as input) data objects.</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/046.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 46: The purchase order document serves as an input to the stock availability check. Based on the outcome of this check, the status of th…\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 46/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>The purchase order document serves as an input to the stock availability check. Based on the outcome of this check, the status of the document is updated, either to “approved” or “rejected”. If the order is approved, an invoice and a shipment notice are produced.<br>Order-to-cash process, again<br>46</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/047.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 47: Model with information artifacts\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 47/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>Model with information artifacts<br>47<br>Beware: This diagram is a too detailed. It is for illustration purposes.<br>In practice, try to only model the most important data objects and associations. Keep the model readable.</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/048.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 48: A Text Annotation is a mechanism to provide additional text information to the model reader\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 48/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>A Text Annotation is a mechanism to provide additional text information to the model reader<br>Doesn’t affect the flow of tokens through the process<br>A Final Note: BPMN Text Annotations<br>48</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/BPS301/flm1/d03/049.webp\" alt=\"BPS301 · Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 49: BPMN Poster (link in “Readings” page)\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">Lecture3-ProcessModeling1 · slide 49/49</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>BPMN Poster (link in “Readings” page)<br>49</p></div>\n<div class=\"ml-en\"><div class=\"note-ct\">📝 Detailed lecture and 🎬 video for this session: coming later. For now follow the slides, the materials and the tasks above.</div></div>\n<div class=\"ml-vi\"><div class=\"note-ct\">📝 Bài giảng chi tiết và 🎬 video cho buổi này: sẽ bổ sung sau. Trước mắt học theo slide, tài liệu và nhiệm vụ ở trên.</div></div>"
